---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Grayson, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, or storm water in Grayson, GA 30017. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Gwinnett County</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water where it doesn't belong? We're here to help. Call or click "Request
      help in Grayson" now and fill out the form. Share as much as you can about
      the problem and we will get back to you as soon as we can.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Grayson</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Grayson</h2>
    <p>
      Most people who say they live in Grayson do not live in the city. The city
      of Grayson is tiny, about 4,700 people. Most of the houses with a Grayson
      address are in unincorporated Gwinnett County, in subdivisions out along
      Highway 20, Rosebud Road, and Pharrs Road. State Route 20 is even called
      two things. It is Grayson Highway on one side of town and Loganville
      Highway on the other.
    </p>
    <p>
      The big subdivisions are mostly from the 2000s. Wheatfields and Great River
      at Tribble Mill were built then, with two- and three-story houses and
      swim-and-tennis clubs. So many homes here are 20 to 25 years old. The water
      heater and the hoses from those first years are now old, which is why the
      usual call is a leak, not a flood.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is under the house matters. Some of the 2000s houses have basements,
      and the older houses in and around the town are more likely to sit over a
      crawl space. On a slab, water spreads across the floor. Tell us what you
      have, and if you are not sure, say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      Grayson does have a river, the Alcovy. The gauge people hear about on the
      news is the one at New Hope Road. Flood stage is 9 feet. During Hurricane
      Helene in 2024, it reached about 10.6 feet, which the weather service
      called minor flooding. At 11 feet, the weather service says the water
      reaches the bottom of the bridge. That number is for the river at the
      bridge. It does not measure your street, and most houses here are not next
      to the river.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the first questions are about your house. When you call or send
      the form, tell us where the water came from, which rooms are wet, and how
      long it has been there. Also tell us your street and nearest crossroad.
      Mail addresses around here can be confusing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms do some damage. In a January 2022 storm, a tree came down at
      Chandler Road and Tribble Creek Drive. A damaged roof can leak into the
      attic for days before the stain shows up on the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>Grayson has one ZIP code, 30017.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Uptown and the Railyard.</strong> The old town center, with new
      shops and restaurants. Older houses nearby.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Wheatfields and Wheatfields Reserve.</strong> Craftsman and
      traditional two- and three-story houses from the 2000s, with a
      swim-and-tennis club. Some have basements.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Great River at Tribble Mill.</strong> About 525 houses from the
      2000s, near Tribble Mill Park. Tribble Mill is a county park, not a city
      park.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pharrs Road.</strong> A mix of older and newer houses.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Highway 20 corridor.</strong> Everything, from older houses to
      newer streets. It is not one neighborhood.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Grayson</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage or closet.
        This is common in houses from the 2000s.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. Gwinnett had a lot of these in December 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Outdoor faucet or irrigation line.</strong> A broken line near the
        house can send water along the foundation and into a basement or crawl
        space.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm water.</strong> Rain gets in through a window, a door, or a
        roof damaged by wind or a falling tree.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Grayson homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>
          My mail says one town, but I live near Grayson. What address do I give?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Give the street address and ZIP exactly as they are on your mail, and
          then tell us the nearest crossroad, such as Rosebud Road, Pharrs Road,
          or Highway 20. Mailing towns around here do not always match where you
          live, and Highway 20 changes names from one side of town to the other.
          That helps the crew find the right house.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          The news said the Alcovy is at 10 feet. Does that mean my house will
          flood?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Not by itself. That number is for the river at the New Hope Road
          bridge. It does not measure your street. If you live near the river,
          watch the gauge when heavy rain is coming, and move anything valuable up
          off the floor. Ask your insurer about outside water coverage before the
          storm, not after. If water does get in, take photos and call.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>An outdoor faucet froze and water ran inside the wall. What do I do?</dt>
        <dd>
          Shut off the main valve first. A split faucet can run for hours before
          anyone sees it, and the water goes into the wall and under the floor.
          Take photos, do not open the wall yourself, and call. Tell us how long
          it may have been running.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          A sprinkler line broke near the house. Can that cause water damage
          inside?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Yes. If you have an irrigation system, a broken line near the foundation
          can send water along the wall and into a basement or crawl space. Turn
          off the irrigation system, look for wet carpet or a musty smell in the
          lower level, and call if you find water.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer backup. Say which one it is on
      the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, outside water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="grayson" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/lawrenceville">Lawrenceville</a> ·
      <a href="/snellville">Snellville</a> ·
      <a href="/dacula">Dacula</a> ·
      <a href="/loganville">Loganville</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
